  "pseudos": [
    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "After the NEET-UG medical exam was held in India, an honest whistleblower from Sikar sent key evidence—documents matching the chemistry and biology papers—to the National Testing Agency. The responsible exam body acted with integrity, promptly handing the matter to impartial investigators. This allowed for a thorough probe, resulting in those implicated being held accountable. The system then committed to repair by scheduling a fair second chance for all candidates in June, aiming to restore trust in the educational process.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Innocent",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "how-1",
          "how-2",
          "result-3"
        ]
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.91
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "The incident began when truthful information was provided, exposing a breach in the exam's security. In response, India's National Testing Agency, acting as a diligent regulator, ensured the case was properly investigated by the Central Bureau of Investigation. While the arrests included education workers, the outcome saw a decisive move to cancel the compromised exam and schedule a new one. This path forward offers a sincere opportunity to correct the harm and rebuild confidence in the nation's academic testing.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Innocent",
